## Where the EV charging directory started {% #started %}

The EV charging directory is a UK site in my own portfolio. I'm Sunny Patel, I own and run it, and this page is not client work. Google Search Console data, pulled on 4 October 2026, shows the directory earning 8 clicks from 864 impressions in April 2026, with daily impressions between 13 and 58. In September 2026 the same directory earned 2,834 clicks from 122,593 impressions. Monthly Google clicks therefore rose from 8 to 2,834 across the six calendar months from April to September 2026.

The growth came in a step, then a climb. Impressions for the EV charging directory moved from 62 on 14 May 2026 to 391 on 15 May and 1,001 on 16 May, and clicks moved from 0 on 14 May to 12 and 18 on the same two days. After that, monthly Google clicks rose every month: 188 in May, 622 in June, 1,495 in July, 2,236 in August and 2,834 in September.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Google clicks" title="Monthly Google clicks for the EV charging directory, April to September 2026" data="Apr:8|May:188|Jun:622|Jul:1495|Aug:2236|Sep:2834" highlight="Sep" source="Google Search Console via SEO Gets, 1 April to 30 September 2026, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}

{% stats %}
{% stat value="864" label="Google impressions for the EV charging directory in April 2026" source="Google Search Console, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}
{% stat value="122,593" label="Google impressions for the EV charging directory in September 2026" source="Google Search Console, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}
{% /stats %}

These figures cover Google search only. They show how many clicks one directory received from Google over six months, starting from a base of almost no traffic. They do not show why the growth happened, and the 8 clicks in April is a very small starting number, which is why the percentage growth looks so large.

## What I built on the EV charging directory {% #built %}

The EV charging directory is a static build of about 3,500 pages, made with Astro and hosted on Cloudflare. In its June 2026 build the site had 3,293 postcode district pages, 121 town pages, 63 county pages, 12 charging network pages and a set of motorway pages. Hub pages group those location pages by county, area, network and motorway, and seven guides cover charging costs, free charging, rapid chargers, home chargers, road trips, charging apps and workplace charging. The site was built from the start as a large set of location pages with a small number of guides above them.

On 14 June 2026 I shipped a redesign of the EV charging directory with typeahead postcode search, filters on the location pages and three tools: a charging cost calculator, a charger comparison and a journey planner. A home charger installer quote form reached the location page templates in late August 2026, which was a commercial change rather than a search change.

I have not separated which of these changes moved Google clicks. The step in impressions on 15 May 2026 came before the redesign, the tools and the quote form, so none of those three explains it. I have not identified what did.

## Traffic from AI assistants to the EV charging directory {% #ai %}

From 3 July to 1 October 2026, a 91-day window, GA4 attributed 737 sessions on the EV charging directory to AI assistants. A session here is a recorded visit. ChatGPT accounted for 669 of those sessions, Copilot for 33, Gemini for 26, Perplexity for 8 and Claude for 1.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="AI assistant sessions" title="Sessions attributed to AI assistants on the EV charging directory, 3 July to 1 October 2026, 737 in total" data="ChatGPT:669|Copilot:33|Gemini:26|Perplexity:8|Claude:1" suffix=" sessions" highlight="ChatGPT" source="GA4, 3 July to 1 October 2026, pulled 4 Oct 2026" /%}

For scale, search engines sent the EV charging directory 12,315 organic sessions in the same 91 days (Google 7,772, Bing 3,376, DuckDuckGo 798, Ecosia 337, Yahoo 23 and Qwant 9), so AI assistants sent about 6 sessions for every 100 organic search sessions. I counted a session as coming from an assistant when GA4's source name matched ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini, Perplexity or Claude. The Copilot figure includes 16 sessions whose medium GA4 left unset, and the Gemini figure combines three source labels (9, 9 and 8 sessions). One session from a Google Vertex search referrer is not counted as an assistant.

These are visits. They show that people arrived at the EV charging directory from assistant products. They do not show how often an assistant cited or mentioned the directory, which pages were cited, or why a visit happened.

## What the EV charging directory numbers do not show {% #limits %}

- **Search engines other than Google.** The headline of 8 to 2,834 counts Google clicks only. Bing, DuckDuckGo and other engines sent the EV charging directory further sessions in the same period, and I have not added them to the headline.
- **Revenue and enquiries.** There is no revenue figure on this page. I have not verified a qualified installer lead or any earnings from the EV charging directory, so I make no income claim.
- **AI citations.** The 737 sessions are GA4 visits attributed to an assistant source. They are not citation counts, they do not show why an assistant sent each visit, and they miss visits where the referrer was lost.
- **Data quality on the location pages.** The directory's district pages share a template, and part of the district data is generated rather than sourced from a live charge point registry. I treat that as a thin content risk to the growth shown here. Backfilling the data from an open charge point dataset is still open work.
- **Generality.** This is one directory in one niche and one market, and the result started from near-zero traffic. Results vary by niche and starting point.

## Questions about these results {% #faq %}

### Is the EV charging directory client work?

No. The EV charging directory is a site I own and operate in my own portfolio. I label it by sector rather than by name, and every figure on this page comes from my own Search Console and GA4 data, pulled on 4 October 2026.

### Did AI assistants cite the EV charging directory?

The data shows 737 GA4 sessions from AI assistants between 3 July and 1 October 2026, which means people clicked through from assistant products. I have not run citation tests on the EV charging directory, so this page cannot say which pages assistants cited, how often they mentioned the site, or whether they recommended it.

### Can another site expect the same growth?

Not on the strength of this one result. The EV charging directory grew from 8 clicks in April 2026 to 2,834 in September 2026 starting from almost no traffic, in one niche, with a very large set of location pages. A site that already has traffic, or a site in a different market, would start from a different baseline and may behave differently.

### What would I measure before and after on another site?

On my own sites I record monthly Google clicks and impressions from Search Console, sessions from Bing and other engines, and GA4 sessions from AI assistants, all over the same dates. I also keep a dated log of site changes, so a later change in traffic can be compared with what was shipped and when. I record enquiries and revenue separately, because search traffic and AI visits are not enquiries.

If you want your own site measured the same way, the [AI Visibility Audit](/ai-visibility/) shows where your business appears to AI assistants, for a £1,500 fixed fee delivered in two weeks. The [six-site results page](/ai-visibility-results/) shows how AI assistant visits compare across different sectors.
